  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic** with confidence 0.85, using 40 of the 60 budget. Learning cost followed how similar a task's input-output mapping was to trained tasks. It did not follow whether the task's rules had been learned before.\n\n**Phase 1: each rule trained once.** I trained L1-S1-M1, L2-S2-M2 and L3-S3-M3. Then I tested tasks that recombine already-trained rules (0 new rules) against L4-S4-M3, which has 2 new rules. All of them had about the same similarity to the trained tasks (0.5\u20130.625).\n- The recombinations took about 20 examples on average.\n- L4-S4-M3 took about 22.\n\nA part-based learner should have learned the recombinations clearly faster, and it didn't.\n\n**Phase 2: making rules eligible to become parts.** I trained 9 more tasks so that L4, S4 and M4 each appeared in 3 trained tasks. That meets the threshold whether k is 2 or 3. I picked these tasks to stay dissimilar to L4-S4-M4: the highest similarity was 0.375.\n- L4-S4-M4 did not get cheaper. Before phase 2 it took 20 and 31. After, it took 37, 22, 54, 37, 1, 35, 51, 16 (mean about 32). Under factor-when-justified, all three of its rules would now be reusable parts, so it should have become cheap.\n- L1-S1-M3 also has all its rules trained 3 or more times, but it is 0.75 similar to a trained task. It was cheap: 23, 12, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1.\n\nBoth tasks are made entirely of rules the learner has seen repeatedly, and only the similar one was learned quickly. That points to holistic over both other hypotheses.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- The test results are very noisy, including occasional values of 1, so phase 1 alone would have been weak evidence.\n- The phase 2 contrast is the strongest evidence. It rests on 8 samples of L4-S4-M4 and 7 of L1-S1-M3.\n- The tasks that reuse M4 could not be made less than 0.375 similar to L4-S4-M4, so \"low similarity\" there means 0.375, not near zero.",
